is  what you want is you want to have  species that are well spaced out from  each other and you want to have even  biomass across all of the species in  your mixture so this is actually if all  of this  biomass was even this would be a fairly  divergent mixture when we go to the four  species mix actually adding this canola  in the middle here that reduces our  divergence okay because we've put a  species in there that has traits that  are very close to the middle and we want  traits that are evenly spaced across the  edge so this is actually a case where  adding a fourth species if you're  thinking about species diversity is as  your indicator adding the fourth species  actually reduced our functional  diversity here so I think there's some  traps that we could fall into when we  don't understand functional diversity  you might think oh let me add four or  five six species well here's another  good example of where having some  species in the middle of this volume  reduces the divergence so I think  increasing our literacy around  functional diversity will help us create  better mixtures but higher performing  mixtures  okay so challenge number two you now  have to have evenness spread out around  the edges of your trait space which  means you need to manage cover crop  mixtures to be even in their biomass  production and this is a figure that  shows biomass or essentially the  composition of the cover crop stand over  time for the four different mixtures  here so we have the composition at  seeding so this is plants per meter  squared we have the composition at  germination so the number of plants that  germinated per meter squared our fall  biomass composition and our spring  biomass composition for the the  different mixtures and each of these  shaded areas different colors represents  a different species in the mix and what  we saw is that across all of the  mixtures is in spring we had a strong  convergence towards cereal rice cereal  rye really dominated a lot of our mixes  we lost red clover was really a minor  component you know so we really didn't  achieve very even mixtures in in this  first stage of the experiment this is a  picture of the three species nitrogen  here in spring the first year of the  experiment so the cereal rye you see  very tall here red clover is you know  just hanging on down there a little bit  of Austrian winter pea so that was it  that was a big challenge for evenness  and we knew in the second phase of this  experiment we needed to do something  about the evenness so we switched up our  species that we used in these mixes and  in in the the second phase of the  experiment we shifted RIE to triticale  and we shifted red clover to crimson  clover so if you know anything about rye  and red clover rye is the fastest  growing of the grasses in the spring red  clover is the slowest growing of the  legumes in the spring and so we set this  we basically set this mixture up for  failure because of the differences in  spring growth rates so now we're going  to a slower growing grass the triticale  and a faster growing clover the crimson  clover and we're seeing much more even  biomass in the stands another challenge  for evenness is that the expression of  these mixtures is very different across  different farm sites because of the soil  fertility and the climate at each site  so this is the four species mixture  planted four different locations these  three were on farm locations and so this  shows the the biomass composition in the  fall and you see depending on these  different sites like this site was  almost all canola this was all kunal  most all canola but a good bit of pea  here we had canola and rye here it was  mostly rye a little bit of canola and  pea  so the factors that we think are driving  this are the temperate the climate and  the nitrogen status so in the second  phase of our work we've really focused  on looking at gradients of climate and  nitrogen status at seeding to be able to  predict how the biomass might shift and  try and adjust our seeding rates so a  postdoc on the project Barbara barabar  has a poster this evening looking at  some of this work where we're  mechanistically changing planting dates  and nitrogen fertility  looking at differences in biomass  composition so we'd welcome you to stop  by that okay when we think another  challenge when we think about you know  doing a spider plot like this the just  that the layout of this spider plot  implicitly implies that these services  are somewhat independent of each other  and that it might actually be possible  to have you know high service levels on  all of these around you know if you  theoretically have the optimal mixture  what we saw from the data in the first  phase of this experiment this is a  principal components analysis with all  of our services and this type of  analysis what it does is it allows you  to see what services are associated with  each other which services are opposed  with each other which services are  independent of each other and so the way  things shook out is we have these  bundles of services so nitrogen supply  cash crop production and profitability  were all related to each other so this  is a service bundle here and a different  bundle of services were actually opposed  so there was a trade-off so nitrogen  retention weed suppression and biomass  production these were somewhat clustered  together these are all opposed to each  other so there's a trade-off between  these services soil carbon and pest  suppression seem to be relatively  independent so how do we how do we  manage this trade-off well in all of the  data that we have from on-farm  experiments and research station  experiments we were able to with the  nitrogen trade-off at least start to  understand what's driving this trade-off  so we have cover crop management factors  that are affecting nitrogen supply and  nitrogen retention and there's also soil  management factors and for the c